After thinking some more: is the explanation that `rake db:migrate` will run before the final code is deployed, so if both are done in a single deploy: 
1. there is a narrow window after `db:migrate` where old code is still running and can access the removed column;
2. the deploy can fail, but the migration won't be rolled back by default, so again we can end up with old code trying to access the removed column?

@sbdchd thanks for the quick response! ❤️ 

Personally, I think 1-4 make sense by default, but I would make the 5th optional. 

Are you happy for us to write up a PR?

body:
Here's a migration generated by Django that doesn't generate a warning from Squawk

```sql
BEGIN;
--
-- Alter field col_name on foo_tabl
--
ALTER TABLE "foo_tbl" ALTER COLUMN "col_name" SET DEFAULT false;
UPDATE "foo_tbl" SET "col_name" = false WHERE "col_name" IS NULL;
ALTER TABLE "foo_tbl" ALTER COLUMN "col_name" SET NOT NULL;
ALTER TABLE "foo_tbl" ALTER COLUMN "col_name" DROP DEFAULT;
COMMIT;
```

code change in python land was:


```python
class Foo(Model):
    field_name = models.BooleanField(null=True)
```

being replaced by:

```python
class Foo(Model):
    field_name = models.BooleanField(null=False, default=False)
```

comments:
I think this is the proper alternative
```sql
ALTER TABLE "foo_tbl" ALTER COLUMN "col_name" SET DEFAULT false;

-- prevent new null rows
ALTER TABLE "foo_tbl" ADD CONSTRAINT col_name_null
    CHECK ("col_name" IS NOT NULL) NOT VALID;

-- backfill column so it's not null
UPDATE "foo_tbl" SET "col_name" = false WHERE "col_name" IS NULL;
-- verify existing columns are not-null
ALTER TABLE "foo_tbl" VALIDATE CONSTRAINT col_name_null;
-- remove default
ALTER TABLE "foo_tbl" ALTER COLUMN "col_name" DROP DEFAULT;
```

@chdsbd I took a stab at using that, but the `CommentStmt` is for an actual SQL `COMMENT ON` https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-comment.html

The downside of using this is that it actually changes the DB schema and you can only have one comment per object, so it would overwrite any existing comment on the object.

Hopefully there is a way to get the actual code comment i.e. `-- I am an SQL comment`
